const e="2026-10-26",t=[{title:"Overshoot Is Not Surrender: The UN's 2026 Emissions Gap Report and the Narrow Path Back to 1.5°C",source:"UN Environment Programme, Emissions Gap Report 2026: The Point of Return, published 21 October 2026 (reported by Reuters, 21 October 2026)",passage:`The chance of holding global warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels with no or limited overshoot — the most ambitious goal of the Paris Agreement — is now gone. That is the stark conclusion of the Emissions Gap Report 2026: The Point of Return, published on 21 October by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP). Yet the report insists that overshoot need not be permanent: by dramatically reducing greenhouse gas emissions and removing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, humanity can minimise peak temperatures and chart a path back to 1.5 degrees by the end of the century. It is, in the words of UN Secretary-General António Guterres, a message that overshoot is not surrender.

The report sketches what it calls the "lowest possible" overshoot scenario. In this pathway, global temperatures peak at around 1.7 to 1.8 degrees above pre-industrial levels by around the middle of the century, then gradually decline to 1.5 degrees or below by 2100. Achieving it would require emissions to fall by 18 per cent by 2030, 45 per cent by 2035 and 69 per cent by 2040, compared with 2025 levels — a pace of reduction far beyond anything yet achieved. Even this best remaining outcome offers only roughly a fifty-fifty chance of returning to 1.5 degrees, and it depends heavily on large-scale carbon removal, the feasibility of which the report itself describes as uncertain.

Current national plans fall far short of that trajectory. Full implementation of countries' latest climate pledges, known as nationally determined contributions, would deliver reductions of just 5 to 8 per cent by 2030 and 11 to 15 per cent by 2035. On current policies, UNEP projects warming of 2.8 degrees by 2100; if every pledge were honoured in full, the figure would still reach 2.6 degrees. Both estimates are higher than last year's projections, a deterioration the report attributes partly to the withdrawal of the United States from the Paris Agreement and partly to revised assessments of historical emissions.

The underlying emissions trend explains why the gap is widening rather than closing. Global greenhouse gas emissions reached an estimated 58.4 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent in 2025, broadly unchanged from the previous year. Although preliminary estimates hint at a marginal decline, UNEP cautions that uncertainty in the data makes it too early to conclude that emissions have peaked. Carbon dioxide from fossil fuel combustion continued to rise, alongside emissions of nitrous oxide and fluorinated gases, offset only by lower emissions from land-use change and forestry, partly reflecting reduced wildfire activity compared with 2024.

The stakes of a larger overshoot are spelled out in the report's discussion of impacts. Every additional fraction of a degree increases the severity of heatwaves, the destructive force of storms and the pace of sea-level rise, while pushing ecosystems such as coral reefs and tropical forests closer to thresholds beyond which recovery becomes impossible. Adaptation, the report notes, can blunt some of this damage, but its costs rise steeply with temperature, and for the most vulnerable nations — low-lying island states and drought-prone regions — there are limits to what adaptation alone can achieve.

The timing of the report is deliberate. It lands weeks before COP31, the next round of UN climate talks, which opens in Turkey on 9 November, and it frames the negotiation in unusually blunt terms: the world has spent decades postponing cuts and is now facing the consequences, in the form of deadly floods, brutal heatwaves and surging wildfires. Yet Anne Olhoff, Director of the UNEP-Copenhagen Climate Centre, argues that the goals of the Paris Agreement remain attainable if countries not only meet but surpass their most ambitious targets. The longer the wait, the report warns, the larger the risk that the temperature goal is jeopardised — and the greater the eventual reliance on unproven technologies to undo damage that could have been prevented.`,notes:[{word:"overshoot",pos:"n.",meaning:"超标；超出（目标）"},{word:"stark",pos:"adj.",meaning:"严酷的；明显的"},{word:"minimise",pos:"v.",meaning:"使最小化；尽量减少"},{word:"trajectory",pos:"n.",meaning:"轨迹；发展趋势"},{word:"pledge",pos:"n.",meaning:"承诺；保证"},{word:"deterioration",pos:"n.",meaning:"恶化；退化"},{word:"preliminary",pos:"adj.",meaning:"初步的；预备的"},{word:"marginal",pos:"adj.",meaning:"微小的；边缘的"},{word:"feasibility",pos:"n.",meaning:"可行性"},{word:"jeopardise",pos:"v.",meaning:"危及；使陷入危险"},{word:"deliberate",pos:"adj.",meaning:"故意的；深思熟虑的"},{word:"brutal",pos:"adj.",meaning:"残酷的；严酷的"},{word:"attainable",pos:"adj.",meaning:"可达到的；可获得的"},{word:"offset",pos:"v.",meaning:"抵消；补偿"}],questions:[{q:"What is the central conclusion of the Emissions Gap Report 2026?",options:["Global warming can still be held to 1.5°C with no overshoot","Holding warming to 1.5°C with no or limited overshoot is no longer possible","Emissions have already peaked and are falling fast","The Paris Agreement targets have been abandoned"],answer:1},{q:"In the report's 'lowest possible' overshoot scenario, when do temperatures return to 1.5°C or below?",options:["By 2035","By around the middle of the century","By 2100","They never return to 1.5°C"],answer:2},{q:"By how much must global emissions fall by 2035, compared with 2025 levels, in that scenario?",options:["18 per cent","45 per cent","69 per cent","5 to 8 per cent"],answer:1},{q:"What would full implementation of current national pledges deliver by 2030?",options:["A reduction of 18 per cent","A reduction of 45 per cent","A reduction of just 5 to 8 per cent","A reduction of 69 per cent"],answer:2},{q:"On current policies, UNEP projects warming of 2.8°C by 2100.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"The report confirms that global emissions have definitely peaked.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"The report states that COP31 will be held in Turkey.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"According to the report, carbon removal technologies are already proven at the required scale.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1}]},{title:"Mapping the Body's Hidden Geography: 'Satellite Maps' of Proteins Point to New Alzheimer's Targets",source:"MedicalXpress, 24 October 2026, reporting research from the University at Buffalo published in Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (2026)",passage:`Drug discovery has long suffered from a problem of geography. Researchers may know that a particular protein is implicated in a disease, yet have only a vague idea of where that protein sits within a tissue, which neighbouring molecules it interacts with, or whether a drug designed to attack it can actually reach it. A new technique developed at the University at Buffalo promises to change that, by creating what its inventors describe as satellite maps of thousands of proteins across an entire tissue — and simultaneously tracking where an antibody drug travels inside the brain.

The method, called hex-MASP, maps the whole-tissue spatial proteome: the full cast of proteins present in a sample, located with the precision of a street map rather than a mere inventory. In a study published in Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, the team used it to chart the distribution of proteins across brain tissue and, crucially, to follow the intrabrain distribution of monoclonal antibodies — the class of laboratory-made molecules that underpins many modern therapies. Knowing precisely where such an antibody goes, and which proteins it encounters on the way, could determine whether a candidate drug succeeds or fails long before it reaches a patient.

The practical value, the researchers argue, lies in earlier and better decisions. That information could help scientists improve a drug candidate or reconsider its target before committing to costly clinical trials, the stage at which most experimental medicines fail and where each failure can consume years and hundreds of millions of dollars. Using hex-MASP, the laboratory has already identified promising new candidates for drug targets in Alzheimer's disease, a condition that has proven stubbornly resistant to treatment despite decades of research and repeated late-stage disappointments.

The advance matters because conventional protein analysis typically destroys exactly the information researchers need most. Grinding up a tissue sample yields a list of its contents, but erases all knowledge of which proteins sat beside which, and in which cells. By preserving spatial context, hex-MASP restores that lost dimension: a protein that appears harmless in one neighbourhood of a tissue may prove decisive in another, and a drug that reaches one region of the brain may never arrive in the region where it is needed. The technique thus converts a flat inventory into a three-dimensional picture of disease as it actually unfolds in the body.

The context is a field badly in need of better maps. Alzheimer's disease affects tens of millions of people worldwide, and its toll is projected to rise steeply as populations age, yet effective treatments remain scarce and the history of drug development in this area is littered with expensive failures. Many of those failures, researchers suspect, were not failures of chemistry but of targeting — the right molecule aimed at the wrong place, or at a protein whose role was misunderstood. A tool that clarifies where targets sit and where drugs travel could therefore shorten one of medicine's longest and costliest journeys from laboratory bench to bedside.

The work is the product of a broad collaboration. The team includes Min Ma, a research assistant professor of pharmaceutical sciences, Ming Zhang, a research scientist, Shihan Huo, a former postdoctoral researcher who is now a principal scientist at Regeneron, and Chi Zhou, a professor of industrial and systems engineering. Their ambition, in the words of the laboratory's lead, is to help researchers discover better targets and develop treatments that make a greater difference for patients — beginning with diseases such as Alzheimer's that have proven hardest to treat, but extending, in time, to more diseases and more kinds of medicine.

The analogy with satellite mapping is apt. Just as a satellite image reveals not only individual buildings but the roads, rivers and boundaries that connect them, a spatial map of proteins reveals the architecture of a tissue: which cells sit beside which, where a disease process is concentrated, and where a therapy might intervene most effectively. For drug hunters accustomed to working with lists of molecules, that shift — from inventory to geography — may prove to be the technique's most consequential contribution.`,notes:[{word:"implicated",pos:"adj.",meaning:"牵涉其中的；与……有关的"},{word:"inventory",pos:"n.",meaning:"清单；存货"},{word:"monoclonal",pos:"adj.",meaning:"单克隆的"},{word:"antibody",pos:"n.",meaning:"抗体"},{word:"underpin",pos:"v.",meaning:"支撑；构成……的基础"},{word:"candidate",pos:"n.",meaning:"候选物；候选人"},{word:"clinical trial",pos:"n.",meaning:"临床试验"},{word:"stubbornly",pos:"adv.",meaning:"顽固地；难以改变地"},{word:"collaboration",pos:"n.",meaning:"合作；协作"},{word:"analogy",pos:"n.",meaning:"类比；比喻"},{word:"architecture",pos:"n.",meaning:"结构；构造"},{word:"intervene",pos:"v.",meaning:"干预；介入"},{word:"consequential",pos:"adj.",meaning:"重要的；有重大影响的"},{word:"cast",pos:"n.",meaning:"全体成员；阵容"}],questions:[{q:"What problem in drug discovery does hex-MASP address?",options:["A lack of new proteins to study","Knowing a protein is linked to a disease but not where it sits in tissue or whether a drug can reach it","The high cost of satellite imaging","A shortage of laboratory animals"],answer:1},{q:"What does hex-MASP map, in addition to proteins?",options:["The electrical activity of neurons","The intrabrain distribution of monoclonal antibodies","The genetic mutations of patients","The blood flow of the whole body"],answer:1},{q:"Why does earlier target information matter, according to the researchers?",options:["It guarantees a drug will be approved","It lets scientists improve a candidate or reconsider its target before costly clinical trials","It replaces clinical trials entirely","It reduces the number of proteins in a tissue"],answer:1},{q:"For which disease has the lab already identified promising new drug targets using hex-MASP?",options:["Parkinson's disease","Alzheimer's disease","Diabetes","Lung cancer"],answer:1},{q:"Hex-MASP provides only a list of proteins, with no information about their locations.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"Shihan Huo is now a principal scientist at Regeneron.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"The passage states that hex-MASP has already cured Alzheimer's disease in patients.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"The researchers intend to extend the technique to more diseases and types of medicine.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0}]}],o={date:e,newsReadings:t};export{e as date,o as default,t as newsReadings};
